{"reference":"Ruth 4","book":"Ruth","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"A public kinship arrangement","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The proceeding at the city gate publicly joins responsibility for family property, preservation of the deceased man's name, and Boaz's marriage to Ruth after the nearer redeemer declines. These concerns overlap with other biblical provisions about land and family continuity, but Ruth 4 should not be treated as describing one identical legal mechanism.","verse_refs":["Ruth 4:1-12","Ruth 4:1-17","Ruth 4:18-22","Ruth 4:5-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_6bc03a9c0c2a9fd77ef21834"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"The closing genealogy","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The women name the child Obed and describe him as Naomi's restorer. The final genealogy then traces Obed through Jesse to David, so the chapter closes by connecting this household's renewed life with a larger family line.","verse_refs":["Ruth 4:13-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_159f45496cc9c548ed878067"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Later interpretation","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Matthew's genealogy explicitly includes Ruth and Boaz. Calling Boaz a direct type of Christ, however, is a later Christian interpretation rather than an identification made explicitly by the New Testament.","verse_refs":["Ruth 4:13-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_052a32fe3f467e7aa60f2a29"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The story's larger movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The ending completes a movement across Ruth from famine, bereavement, and Naomi's emptiness toward harvest, kinship redemption, birth, communal blessing, and genealogy. This helps explain why the women's celebration around Naomi and Obed carries such weight at the close.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1","Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_f9518b9e99587f99357428a9"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Ruth's story also stands beside later passages that address Ammonites and Moabites. Nehemiah 13 cites Deuteronomy 23 in connection with Tobiah's temple access; how that passage relates to Ruth and to texts welcoming faithful foreigners remains a debated interpretive question, rather than grounds for treating it as a universal ethnic ban.","verse_refs":["Ruth 1:1-22","Ruth 2:1-23","Ruth 3:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_2931eaaf6290a4689e4aa39f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Divine action and human action","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The narrative explicitly attributes provision and conception to the LORD, while its events also move through the actions of Naomi, Ruth, Boaz, workers, elders, and the women. Keeping both features in view prevents the chapter from being reduced either to private human initiative or to action without human participation.","verse_refs":["Ruth 4:1-12","Ruth 4:1-17","Ruth 4:18-22","Ruth 4:5-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ruth_004_e62bf349509d71a73d524af0"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}